Title = {Understanding land-use conflicts related to mass tourism in protected
   coastal dunes: an application to conservation management},
Journal = {JOURNAL OF OUTDOOR RECREATION AND TOURISM-RESEARCH PLANNING AND
   MANAGEMENT},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {55},
Month = {SEP},
Abstract = {Tourism activities in sensitive environments, such as dune fields or
   beaches, can be a cause of complex conflicts between development and
   conservation. The Maspalomas Dunes Special Nature Reserve (Gran Canaria,
   Spain) has received millions of visitors since the 1990s, attracted by a
   combination of year-round sunshine, warm weather, dune landscape, and a
   wide range of recreational uses. The urbanization of the surroundings of
   the Reserve and the occupation of sections of the beach-dune system by
   kiosks and other equipment, together with regular ``beach cleaning{''}
   using heavy machinery, constantly disturb and put pressure on this
   iconic site. Also, visitors entering the dune field often leave an
   important environmental footprint when they do not follow local
   environmental rules. This study the impacts associated with visitors
   that enter the dune field to go off-track and wander around protected
   and sensitive areas. Surveys were carried out in situ to understand
   motivations of people for leaving official tracks. Additional field work
   consisting of mapping environmental damages helped understanding the
   spatial distribution and characteristics of dune degraded areas
   ({''}degraded spots{''}). Results show marked differences between user
   expectations when visiting the reserve vs. goals set by local
   environmental management. Common visitors' activities (e.g., partying)
   were completely at odds with the actual legal framework for the
   environmental protection of the dune field (e.g., habitats protection).
   The discussion reviews current conflicts and the need to adapt public
   uses to the carrying capacity of the dune ecosystem. We argue that the
   prevailing tourist-centric business-model in Maspalomas, which
   disregards fundamental principles of environmental conservation, poses
   significant detriment to local habitats, species, and the
   geomorphological integrity of this unique environment. A
   multi-stakeholder approach for conflict resolution could help management
   to strike a balance between public access and the need for meaningful
   environmental protection.},
