Abstract = {Understanding how psychological constructs shape farmers' views on
   agri-environmental policy and the role of pollinators in agriculture can
   help design effective and legitimate agricultural policies. While
   farmers are key actors in pollinator conservation, recent farmer
   protests indicate that their support for agri-environmental policies
   such as the EU Common Agricultural Policy eco-schemes is uneven and
   contested. This study examines how two psychological
   constructs-environmental attitudes and psychological reactance-relate to
   farmers' views on the role of pollinators for agriculture and
   agri-environmental policies. We conducted face-to-face surveys with 248
   farmers in Spain and analysed the effect of the psychological constructs
   and farming variables using multiple linear regression. Farmers
   generally acknowledged the importance of pollinators for nature and
   agriculture and were aware of the impacts of agriculture on pollinators.
   Many rejected the idea that their own farming practices contribute to
   pollinators' decline. Views on the Common Agricultural Policy and
   eco-schemes were ambivalent. While some farmers valued the Common
   Agricultural Policy and eco-schemes' potential to promote
   sustainability, others viewed them as economically harmful, too rigid
   and designed without considering their opinion. Positive environmental
   attitudes were associated with the recognition of the value of
   pollinators and support for agri-environmental policy. In contrast,
   psychological reactance was negatively associated with
   agri-environmental policy but unrelated to the recognition of
   pollinators' value. These findings suggest that awareness of pollinator
   contribution and resistance to top-down regulations operate as distinct
   forces shaping farmers' responses to agri-environmental policy. These
   effects held across multiple analytical methods and were robust to
   sociodemographic and farming system covariates. Our results highlight
   the importance of addressing both positive (environmental attitudes) and
   negative (psychological reactance) psychological constructs when
   designing agri-environmental policies. Farmers' reactance may undermine
   engagement with agri-environmental policies, even if they agree with the
   importance of pollinator conservation, suggesting that policy acceptance
   depends not only on what environmental goals are pursued but also on how
   they are designed and implemented.Read the free for this article on the
   Journal blog.
